WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
We explain creativity in the workplace.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.000
This is Sean, a product manager working on the launch of a new sailboat.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.000
The presentation has to be ready by 9 a.m. tomorrow.

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.000
Sean feels stressed because he's missing the big idea.

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Sean's supervisor Vincent wants to help him.

6
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.000
He's read that creating spaces lead to creative work.

7
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
So, Vincent decides to build the best workplace on Earth

8
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:35.000
with open-plan offices, common areas, and wellness areas for Sean to be resourceful.

9
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.000
But Sean still doesn't feel creative when he's at work.

10
00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:45.440
Sean has his best ideas outside the office while running, chatting with friends,

11
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.440
listening to music or having a shower.

12
00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:53.690
Like Sean, the majority of people are not creative on the journey.

13
00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:57.690
The main reasons are, too many distractions, having no time,

14
00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.690
being too focused or feeling stressed.

15
00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:04.690
So, is it ever possible to have outstanding ideas at work?

16
00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:08.420
This is Lisa, Vincent's daughter.

17
00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:12.420
Lisa works on her startup in a garage with her two friends.

18
00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:16.420
Surprisingly, Lisa has her best ideas in the garage.

19
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.420
Vincent wonders why.

20
00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:23.420
The garage isn't the best workplace on Earth.

21
00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.420
Furthermore, he knows Lisa is stressed, too.

22
00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:30.420
Being an entrepreneur is like jumping from a plane without a parachute

23
00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.420
and trying to figure out how to make one on the way down.

24
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.790
So, why does Lisa have her best ideas in the garage?

25
00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:41.790
What matters most for Lisa is her team.

26
00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:44.790
One, they inspire her every day.

27
00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:48.790
They share their thoughts openly and have respect for their expertise.

28
00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:51.790
Two, there is a creative atmosphere.

29
00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:54.790
It's okay to be yourself and do whatever you want.

30
00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:58.790
Sing out loud, do a workout or daydream.

31
00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:04.790
Three, they know creativity is required for their startup to survive and grow.

32
00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:09.139
How can Vincent help his employees to be creative?

33
00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:12.140
He works in a big corporate balloon.

34
00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:15.140
Vincent realizes that creativity in the workplace

35
00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:20.140
depends more on the people and the energy between them than on the environment.

36
00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:24.140
He identifies four key elements behind Lisa's creativity.

37
00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:29.140
First, surround yourself with people on your wavelength.

38
00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:31.140
Folks you would like to go sailing with.

39
00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.140
These are people you are more likely to communicate with,

40
00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.140
which builds trust.

41
00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.140
Trust is a major element for creativity,

42
00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:41.140
because people are more likely to give honest feedback

43
00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:45.640
and come up with varying ideas.

44
00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.640
Second, allow time to not focus.

45
00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:52.640
Under extreme time pressure, people are likely to focus too hard

46
00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.640
and hold on to a narrow point.

47
00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.640
Not focusing helps to widen the horizon.

48
00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.640
For instance, informal talks with colleagues help to see problems

49
00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.640
from a bird's eye view, offer different perspectives,

50
00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:09.640
and thereby open the horizon for unusual solutions.

51
00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:13.980
Third, demand creativity.

52
00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.980
To accelerate creative thinking, get a genius on board.

53
00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.980
A genius places high standards on himself

54
00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:23.980
is passionate about his job and inspires others

55
00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:27.980
to deliver creative excellence too.

56
00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.980
And finally, have a common purpose.

57
00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:32.980
If people are driven by the same goal,

58
00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:35.980
they help each other to have great ideas.

59
00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:42.740
Six months later, Sean, Vincent, and his new colleagues

60
00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:46.740
go on a sailing trip to test drive the new boat.

61
00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:49.740
Like good friends, they have that time of their life.

62
00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.740
And they keep one promise.

63
00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:53.740
What happens on the sailboat?

64
00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:56.740
Stay on the sailboat.
